  var monsters = [
    { icon: '🧛', label: 'Vampire', sub: 'seduction', title: 'Vampire — the fear of being wanted and consumed', body: 'The vampire is intimate danger: it enters by invitation, feeds through the neck, and often looks aristocratic rather than monstrous. It crystallises anxiety about sexual predation, contagion, and the corrupting power of charisma. Bram Stoker\'s 1897 Dracula emerged during intense Victorian anxiety about foreign influence and disease — the vampire as metaphor for syphilis has been well documented. The vampire\'s power to "infect" and transform victims reflects biological disgust responses to contamination; the seductive element maps onto fears about consent and the corruption of innocence. Cross-cultural vampire myths exist in over 40 cultures.' },
    { icon: '🐺', label: 'Werewolf', sub: 'the beast within', title: 'Werewolf — the beast within', body: 'The werewolf embodies fear of the animal nature beneath civilised behaviour — the idea that any person could, under certain conditions, revert to brutality. Psychologically it maps onto fear of loss of control and fear of men specifically. Clinically, the delusion that one is transforming into a wolf (clinical lycanthropy) is a documented psychiatric symptom, typically associated with psychosis or dissociative states. The fear it channels is fundamentally about unpredictability in social bonds: can the people around you really be trusted not to turn?' },
    { icon: '👻', label: 'Ghost', sub: 'unfinished memory', title: 'Ghost — the unfinished dead', body: 'Ghosts encode fear of death\'s incompleteness — the idea that the dead return if their passing was violent, untimely, or unavenged. Unlike the vampire, the ghost\'s primary emotional register is melancholy and obligation rather than predation. Ghosts store unfinished obligation; they haunt what they could not resolve. Cross-culturally, ghost beliefs are nearly universal; even in secular Western populations, ~20% of adults report belief in ghosts. Freud\'s "uncanny" (das Unheimliche) — the feeling that something familiar has become strange — is most purely activated by ghost narratives: a face that should be absent, looking back.' },
    { icon: '🧟', label: 'Zombie', sub: 'contagion and crowds', title: 'Zombie — contagion, crowds, and loss of self', body: 'The zombie is a 20th-century monster whose cultural dominance tracks the rise of pandemic anxiety, mass media, and crowd psychology. Unlike the vampire (individual, seductive), the zombie threat is statistical: one becomes many, exponentially. The zombie encodes three overlapping fears: disease contagion (the bite that transforms), loss of individual identity and consciousness (the self consumed by the horde), and the horror of recognition — the zombie is always someone you knew. George Romero\'s Night of the Living Dead (1968) explicitly used zombies as a metaphor for racial violence and mindless consumerism. Pandemic fiction, from 28 Days Later to The Walking Dead, is zombie fiction with the allegory made direct.' },
    { icon: '🤡', label: 'Clown', sub: 'the uncanny face', title: 'Clown — the uncanny face', body: 'Coulrophobia became culturally prominent in the 1980s and accelerated with Stephen King\'s IT (1986). The mechanism is the uncanny valley: the face is almost but not quite human, and the painted features eliminate microexpressions — the rapid involuntary facial signals by which we read others\' emotions. The clown\'s true emotional state is illegible, which activates the threat-detection system. Research by Andrew McAndrew found clowns rated as the single most frightening occupational category by participants. The 2016 US "creepy clown" panic showed how quickly coulrophobia can spread via social contagion.' },
    { icon: '🪞', label: 'Double', sub: 'identity made unstable', title: 'The double — identity made unstable', body: 'The doppelganger, the mirror-twin, the uncanny double — a figure of European folklore and literary horror (Poe\'s William Wilson, Dostoevsky\'s The Double, Stevenson\'s Jekyll and Hyde) — encodes a specific existential fear: that identity is not singular, stable, or trustworthy. The double represents the parts of the self the person cannot acknowledge: the shadow, in Jungian terms. Horror using the double — Us (2019), Black Swan (2010) — is effective because the threat cannot be escaped. You cannot run from yourself. The double makes the inside external and the private visible.' },
    { icon: '👁️', label: 'Dark thing', sub: 'threat detection limit', title: 'The thing in the dark — primal threat detection', body: 'The amorphous monster in the dark — Lovecraft\'s nameless horrors, the creature at the edge of vision — works on the most ancient circuit: the threat-detection system\'s response to ambiguous perceptual data. When the visual system cannot fully process an object, the amygdala\'s response is amplified rather than suppressed. Incomplete information = maximum threat. Lovecraft consciously exploited this: his most effective entities are never fully described. The fear is of the unknown itself, which is why it cannot be extinguished by any amount of reassurance.' }
