      body: 'Exposure means systematic, repeated contact with the feared cue in the absence of the anticipated catastrophe. The mechanism is inhibitory learning: the brain learns a new, stronger rule — "I can experience this and be safe" — that competes with the original fear memory. Exposure works better when varied across contexts, with the person\'s expectations tested explicitly ("I predicted my anxiety would reach 90, it reached 65"), and without safety behaviours that prevent full contact with the feared stimulus.',
