  var animalPhobias = [
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-spider', label: 'Arachnophobia', sub: 'spiders', title: 'Arachnophobia — fear of spiders', body: 'The most researched animal phobia, affecting ~5% of people clinically (up to 50% report some discomfort). Preparedness theory applies strongly: in 15 controlled studies, humans learn spider fear faster, resist extinction more, and generalise more readily than fear of flowers or mushrooms. Geographic variation is striking: people raised in spider-poor environments (northern Europe) show higher rates than those from spider-dense environments (Australia), suggesting strong cultural transmission layered on top of biological preparedness. One-session treatment (OST) achieves 80–90% clinical response rates for spider phobia.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-staff-snake', label: 'Ophidiophobia', sub: 'snakes', title: 'Ophidiophobia — fear of snakes', body: 'Cross-culturally the most prevalent animal fear in surveys, with ~30% of adults reporting significant discomfort. LeDoux\'s foundational work on fear conditioning used snakes precisely because of their special status in the prepared learning system. Rhesus monkeys show the same pattern: one exposure to a fearful snake reaction produces a permanent snake phobia. In humans, the fear generalises strongly — rope-like objects, S-curves, and hissing sounds all activate the response. Some neuroimaging studies find enhanced amygdala responses to snake images even in non-phobic people, suggesting a species-wide subclinical preparedness.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-dog', label: 'Cynophobia', sub: 'dogs', title: 'Cynophobia — fear of dogs', body: 'Estimated to affect ~7–9% of adults, typically originating from direct trauma — a bite or frightening encounter in childhood. Unlike snakes and spiders, cynophobia is primarily trauma-induced rather than prepared-learning-based. Children bitten before age 5 have a ~30% probability of developing clinically significant cynophobia. The phobia is particularly disabling in urban environments where dogs are ubiquitous. Famous sufferers include Adolf Hitler (who nevertheless owned German Shepherds) — a reminder that phobias and emotional relationships with the feared object can coexist in complicated ways.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-cat', label: 'Ailurophobia', sub: 'cats', title: 'Ailurophobia — fear of cats', body: 'Less common than cynophobia (estimated 2–3% of adults) but famously associated with several historical figures including Napoleon Bonaparte, Julius Caesar, and Genghis Khan — whose commanders were reportedly paralysed near cats. The fear is often centred on unpredictability: cats switch rapidly between calm and aggressive behaviour and are harder to read than dogs, eliminating the social signals that allow most people to feel safe around domestic animals. The scratching and clawing associated with cats may also engage the blood-injection-injury fear system in some individuals. Ailurophobia is generally highly treatable with graduated exposure therapy.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-bug', label: 'Entomophobia', sub: 'insects', title: 'Entomophobia — fear of insects', body: 'Broad insect fear affects approximately 6% of adults at clinical severity. The disgust component is unusually prominent: insect phobia activates the disgust system (contamination, oral incorporation threat) as much as the fear system. Cockroaches are the most feared insect in Western cultures; bees and wasps (apiphobia) are feared primarily for their sting. The rise of bed bugs in the 2000s correlated with a documented increase in entomophobia clinic referrals — a rare case where a phobia\'s prevalence tracked a genuine epidemiological change.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-dove', label: 'Ornithophobia', sub: 'birds', title: 'Ornithophobia — fear of birds', body: 'Relatively uncommon (~1% of adults) but can be severely disabling given how many public spaces contain birds. Alfred Hitchcock\'s The Birds (1963) is often cited by sufferers as precipitating their phobia — a case study in information-transmission pathways. The fear typically focuses on unpredictable bird movement, the flapping of wings (sudden movement plus noise), and proximity to faces. Public squares and beaches can become impossible because birds are semi-controlled: near humans but not under human rules — the uncanny shift that makes them threatening.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-paw', label: 'Musophobia', sub: 'mice and rats', title: 'Musophobia — fear of mice and rats', body: 'Affects approximately 3–5% of adults; significantly more common in women (roughly 3:1 ratio). Disgust is central: the primary aversion is to unpredictable movement, soft body texture, and association with contamination. Rodent fear often combines disease history with the violation of domestic space — a mouse in a kitchen feels worse than a larger animal outdoors because it means the home boundary has been crossed. The Black Death association with rats remains culturally powerful 700 years later.' },
    { icon: 'fa-solid fa-fish-fins', label: 'Selachophobia', sub: 'sharks', title: 'Selachophobia — fear of sharks', body: 'One of the clearest examples of media-amplified dread risk. Annual shark attacks worldwide: ~70–80, with ~5 fatalities. Annual deaths from falling coconuts: estimated 150. Yet shark fear is viscerally reported by ~25% of regular swimmers. Steven Spielberg\'s Jaws (1975) demonstrably caused a widespread increase in shark phobia and contributed to shark culling programmes that reduced great white populations by an estimated 80% in Australian waters. The ocean amplifies the fear because it removes visibility and control — the danger below perception, moving faster than the human body can respond.' }
